body:
Is it possible for user-space (maliciously or not) to trigger a CPU fault due to a NULL pointer access in the kernel?

It seems that we only need a vCPU cap to inject a vIRQ. However if the thread that injects the vIRQ is not running on the same pCPU of the target vCPU then an IPI is sent to the pCPU owner of the vCPU thread.

`vcpu->vcpuTCB` this pointer might be `NULL` if the user space injected a vIRQ to a vCPU that has no TCB associated. This is not the normal behavior of correct code but user-space should **not be allowed** to provoke this or am I missing something?

```
exception_t invokeVCPUInjectIRQ(vcpu_t *vcpu, unsigned long index, virq_t virq)
{
    if (likely(ARCH_NODE_STATE(armHSCurVCPU) == vcpu)) {
        set_gic_vcpu_ctrl_lr(index, virq);
#ifdef ENABLE_SMP_SUPPORT
    } else if (vcpu->vcpuTCB->tcbAffinity != getCurrentCPUIndex()) {
        doRemoteOp3Arg(IpiRemoteCall_VCPUInjectInterrupt, (word_t)vcpu, index, virq.words[0],      vcpu->vcpuTCB->tcbAffinity);
#endif /* CONFIG_ENABLE_SMP */
    } else {
        vcpu->vgic.lr[index] = virq;
    }

    return EXCEPTION_NONE;
}
```

Out of curiosity, is there a reason why `handleInterruptEntry()` calls `getActiveIRQ()` before doing the `CONFIG_KERNEL_MCS` handling?
```
    irq = getActiveIRQ();
#ifdef CONFIG_KERNEL_MCS
    if (SMP_TERNARY(clh_is_self_in_queue(), 1)) {
        updateTimestamp();
        checkBudget();
    }
```
Shouldn't the MSC handling happen first to avoid billing the thread too much? Checking which interrupt is pending can also wait a few more cycles.

in allocate_bi_frame():

```
    /* create the bootinfo frame object */
    pptr = alloc_region(BI_FRAME_SIZE_BITS); <--allocated BI_FRAME_SIZE_BITS
    if (!pptr) {
        printf("Kernel init failed: could not allocate bootinfo frame\n");
        return 0;
    }
    clearMemory((void*)pptr, PAGE_BITS); <-- clear PAGE_BITS
```

It allocates one size, but clears another. Per chance they are the same so the whole thing didn't blow up.

In file `tool/invocation_header_gen.py`, around line 80:

```
enum sel4_arch_invocation_label {
    {{for label, condition in invocations}}
    ...
    {{label}} = nInvocationLabels,
    ...
    {{endfor}}
    nSeL4ArchInvocationLabels
}
```

On ia32, becasue `seL4_arch_invocations` is an empty list, this will cause `nSeL4ArchInvocationLabels` to be declared as 0, rather than `nInvocationLabels` as intented.
